<section class="band band--night">
  <div class="shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">What it asks of you</p>
    <h2>Noticing, not fixing</h2>
    <div class="split">
      <div>
        <p>Most self-care advice is a list of things to add to a day that is already full. This
        is the opposite: a practice built on the assumption that your body has been reporting
        its state all along and nobody taught you to read it.</p>
        <p>The seven steps are the clinical work Reem does with clients, reduced to something
        you can do alone in a chair. They come out of the same lineage as the therapy:
        <a href="/approach/">Somatic Experiencing, NARM and IFS-informed EMDR</a>, which all
        start from sensation rather than analysis.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>It is not therapy and does not pretend to be. It is a way to arrive at therapy, or at
        the rest of your day, with better information about what is actually going on.</p>
        <p class="quote-band">&ldquo;Be gentle with yourself. Just breathe.&rdquo;</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="shell band">
  <h2>The seven steps</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Find your comfortable space.</strong> Indoors or out, somewhere that invites
    calm. You might hold a stone, a leaf, a shell. Notice how you feel, and change nothing.
    <em>Comfort is where healing begins.</em></li>

    <li><strong>Face the light.</strong> Sit facing east, toward the morning sun. The direction
    is doing symbolic work as much as practical: new beginnings. Picture the light on your face.
    <em>Let the sun guide you.</em></li>

    <li><strong>Return to your heart.</strong> Hand on your chest, feel your breath without
    altering it, for about thirty seconds. <em>Come home to your breath.</em></li>

    <li><strong>Breathe in peace, breathe out stress.</strong> Hand still resting there, say
    softly on the inhale, &ldquo;I know I am breathing in fresh air,&rdquo; and on the exhale,
    &ldquo;I know I am breathing out stress.&rdquo; Three times.
    <em>Breathe in fresh air; breathe out stress.</em></li>

    <li><strong>Pause and feel.</strong> Scan from head to toe. Find the tension, the pressure,
    the ache. Note it; do not fix it. <em>Your body speaks. Listen.</em></li>

    <li><strong>Gently name what you feel.</strong> Label the emotion without judgement, then
    ask what it is trying to tell you, and what is underneath it once the story is set aside.
    <em>Name it to know it.</em></li>

    <li><strong>Ground yourself through your senses.</strong> Still in your spot, hand on your
    chest, facing east: what do you see, hear, smell, taste, touch? Sit with any shift you feel
    for at least seventeen seconds. <em>Your senses ground you in the now.</em></li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section class="band band--sand">
  <div class="shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">Final reflection</p>
    <h2>Just breathe</h2>
    <p>You sat back and observed your inner and outer world, not to fix or analyse it, but
    simply to notice it. Your breath is always with you: your silent guide.</p>
    <p class="quote-band">&ldquo;You are learning to listen.&rdquo;</p>
  </div>
</section>

